WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:03.600
Now we all know what saving a game means.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.980
It's the underlying functionality that allows a player

3
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.799
to save the current state of the game,

4
00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:10.890
close it and come back and resume where they left off.

5
00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.630
But what really constitutes a current state of the game

6
00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.070
is really gonna vary from project to project.

7
00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.309
Thankfully, Unreal offers a flexible save system

8
00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:20.170
that allows us to store a plethora of information

9
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.670
about the game and also allows the player

10
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.769
to have multiple saves for this particular game

11
00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.329
in different slots.

12
00:00:26.329 --> 00:00:28.170
So really the main question is how extensive

13
00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.250
do you need your save game to be?

14
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.090
Saving and loading a game can both be done in native C++

15
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.609
and also in blueprint.

16
00:00:34.609 --> 00:00:36.469
And it's important to note that whilst both methods

17
00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.250
offer a synchronous and asynchronous way

18
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.689
of saving and loading, the native C++

19
00:00:40.689 --> 00:00:43.009
also offers explicit synchronous binary

20
00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:46.100
saving and loading methods.

21
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.679
Saving and loading a game in either native or blueprint

22
00:00:48.679 --> 00:00:51.780
follow a very similar flow and set of steps.

23
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.000
For the purposes of this talk,

24
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.140
we'll be talking about them in very general terms.

25
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.780
However, the Unreal Engine documentation

26
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.420
has got a great example of how you can achieve

27
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.140
both loading and saving either synchronously

28
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.659
or asynchronously.

29
00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:05.379
First thing we need to do is create a new save game object.

30
00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.980
In native C++, this would be done by creating a new class

31
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.629
that inherits from the use save game type.

32
00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:12.849
In blueprint, we can create a new blueprint class,

33
00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:14.810
then select the save game parent class

34
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.170
to base our new blueprint from.

35
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.129
Next, we need to choose what data we wish to store

36
00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:21.430
in our save game.

37
00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.810
For example, we may need to store the last known location

38
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.530
and rotation of the player.

39
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.329
In this case, we would declare two new variables

40
00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:31.090
of F vector and F rotator types.

41
00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.849
Alongside the data that you wish to store,

42
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.290
we also need to define two key pieces of information

43
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:39.079
that will allow us to save and load our save data.

44
00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:40.319
The first is just a string

45
00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:42.200
that represents the save slot name.

46
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.319
And the second is an integer

47
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:47.459
that signifies the save game index.

48
00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:50.099
The gameplay statics library provides three key functions

49
00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:51.540
to both native and blueprint

50
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.579
that allow us to save and load our game.

51
00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.620
And those are the create save game object.

52
00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.060
This function takes in a class for your save game.

53
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.900
This will be the class of your new save game object.

54
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:02.900
And this function will return you

55
00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:06.319
that newly created save game object directly.

56
00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:07.959
Next, we have the save game to slot

57
00:02:07.959 --> 00:02:10.439
and async save game to slot.

58
00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:12.159
These functions require the following data

59
00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:13.120
to save your game.

60
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.180
That would be in the save game object itself,

61
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.360
the save game slot name,

62
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.419
and lastly, the save game index.

63
00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:21.159
Lastly, we have the load game from slot

64
00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:23.780
and the asynchronous load game from slot.

65
00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.539
These functions require both the slot name and the index

66
00:02:26.539 --> 00:02:28.699
in order to retrieve the same game object

67
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:31.939
that holds your saved data.

68
00:02:31.939 --> 00:02:33.860
It really is important to note that it's advised

69
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.780
that you make use of the asynchronous versions

70
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.979
of the save game and load game functions

71
00:02:37.979 --> 00:02:40.460
to avoid any hitches on your game thread.

72
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.139
Furthermore, the asynchronous version

73
00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:43.460
will provide a callback function

74
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.419
which not only return the save game object,

75
00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:46.340
but will also let us know

76
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.939
whether or not the procedure was successful or not.
